Epididymitis
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Gradual onset unilateral scrotal pain, Localized epididymal tenderness, Positive Prehn's sign, Increased epididymal vascularity on Doppler

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-013
Reason for Visit: Severe unilateral scrotal pain and swelling, requiring immediate assessment to rule out testicular torsion and initiate targeted antibiotic therapy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe, worsening unilateral scrotal pain accompanied by profound swelling and dysuria. Urgent after-hours physical examination was strictly necessary to differentiate epididymitis from a surgical emergency such as acute testicular torsion, which requires surgical intervention within a six-hour window to save the testicle. This assessment could not safely be delayed until regular in-hours availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The back of my right testicle is completely swollen, red hot, and hurts terribly when I walk or pee."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a gradual onset of unilateral posterior scrotal pain and swelling progressing over the last 3 days. They describe exquisite tenderness localized specifically to the back of the testicle. They have experienced concurrent dysuria, severe urinary urgency, and increased urinary frequency. A mucopurulent urethral discharge was noted on their underwear this morning. They report a low-grade fever and mild systemic malaise. Pain is slightly relieved by wearing tight, supportive underwear.
Functional Status: Ambulation is markedly impaired due to severe pain caused by the weight of the testicle pulling on the spermatic cord.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the swelling and fear of permanent damage; low social risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.8C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight to avoid pressure on the groin, exhibiting an antalgic, wide-based gait.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Right hemiscrotum is erythematous, indurated, and warm to the touch. Exquisite tenderness is localized strictly to the epididymis on palpation, while the anterior testis is relatively non-tender. A positive Prehn's sign is observed, characterized by a distinct reduction in pain upon physical elevation of the affected hemiscrotum. The ipsilateral cremasteric reflex remains fully intact. A small reactive hydrocele is palpable. Expected investigation results would include positive nucleic acid amplification testing for Chlamydia or Gonorrhoea from a first-catch urine sample.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took oral ibuprofen with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Epididymitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Testicular Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the gradual rather than sudden onset, the intact cremasteric reflex, and a positive Prehn's sign (elevation relieves pain rather than worsening it).

Acute Prostatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the primary pathology being strictly localized to the epididymis without severe perineal pain or high fevers.

Testicular Tumour - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute, painful, and inflammatory nature of the swelling, alongside the infectious voiding symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly painful scrotal swelling with associated infectious lower urinary tract symptoms. The critical imperative of the urgent after-hours visit was to perform a focused genitourinary examination to definitively exclude testicular torsion. The presence of a gradual onset, intact cremasteric reflex, localized posterior tenderness, and pain relief upon scrotal elevation strongly supports an infectious epididymitis etiology. Delaying this assessment would have left a potential testicular torsion undiagnosed, risking irreversible ischemic necrosis of the testicle. Immediate initiation of empirical antibiotics was necessary to arrest the infection and prevent abscess formation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ceftriaxone 500mg stat and commenced oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for 14 days, providing empirical coverage for sexually transmitted pathogens. First-catch urine collected for urgent NAAT. Advised strict scrotal support.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce mechanical traction on the inflamed spermatic cord, the patient is instructed to wear a specific athletic supporter or tightly rolled towel beneath the scrotum when resting. Experiencing immediate, continuous pain relief from this physical elevation will positively reinforce the habit of maintaining strict scrotal support until the inflammation resolves.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Athletic supporter (jockstrap) strongly recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to assess clinical response and review NAAT results for potential contact tracing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the pain suddenly becomes agonizing and severe, if the swelling dramatically increases, or if a high fever with uncontrollable shivering develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is an acute infection of the tube at the back of the testicle; sexual partners must be informed if a sexually transmitted infection is confirmed.
Clinical Course: The acute pain should begin to subside within 48 to 72 hours of starting antibiotics, though a firm, painless lump may persist for several weeks as the inflammation completely heals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the pain suddenly worsens dramatically, or if you develop a high fever and start shaking, you must go immediately to the Emergency Department as the infection could be spreading or an abscess forming.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria have travelled up the urinary tract and infected the tube that stores sperm, causing severe swelling and pain. The injection and tablets will kill the bacteria, and elevating the area will stop gravity from pulling on the inflamed tissues.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire 14-day course of antibiotics without missing a dose. Wear supportive underwear at all times. Abstain from all sexual contact until treatment is complete and results are reviewed.

